L000268	
L000269	Despite the large fluctuations and long $L_c$, QCM-driven transport does not fully flatten the pedestal due to $\alpha_{\phi,n}\approx \pi$ (similarly for $\alpha_{\phi,T_e}$ and $\alpha_{\phi,T_i}$).
L000270	This anti-correlated phase shift, also observed experimentally \cite{grenfell2024multi}, resembles kinetic-shear-Alfv\'en-wave oscillations \cite{scott2021turbulence} and reduces the efficiency of nonlinear transport.
L000271	This behavior can be traced to the linear ballooning eigenproblem that governs the KBM,
L000272	which is derived from the two-fluid model used in \grillix{} and has the form
L000273	\begin{equation} \label{eqn:kbm:sim_eqn}
L000274	    \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} \theta } f_{\theta}\frac{\mathrm{d}\Phi}{\mathrm{d} \theta } = \bigg(
L000275	    C_{0,\theta} + C_{2,\theta} {\omega}^2+ C_{1,\theta} {\omega}+\frac{C_{-1,\theta}}{{\omega}-{\omega}_1}\bigg)\Phi\,,
L000276	\end{equation}
L000277	where $\Phi={\tilde{A}_{\parallel,\mathbf{k}}}/({k_\parallel R_0 })$ is the Fourier eigenfunction to be solved, $\omega=\omega_r+\gamma i$ is the complex eigenfrequency and $\theta$ is the ballooning angle~\cite{connor1978shear}. $f_{\theta}=1+(s \theta-\alpha_m \sin \theta)^2$ is a function of $\theta$ depending on $\alpha_m$ and the magnetic shear $s$.
L000278	Inside the bracket is a polynomial of $\omega^j$ with the prefactor $C_{j,\theta}$, which assembles the key physics determining the instability of the system.
L000279	The $C_{0,\theta}$ and $C_{2,\theta}$ terms recover the ideal ballooning mode \cite{connor1978shear},
L000280	which has a quadratic form of $\omega$ and hence $\omega_r=0$ and the approximated interchange-like phase shift $\alpha_{\phi,n} \approx \arg(\omega) = \pi/2$.
L000281	The $C_{-1,\theta}$ term represents the ion magnetic drift resonance, originating from the ion's perpendicular heat conduction as a two-fluid effect in toroidal geometry, and destabilizes the system by reducing $\alpha_m$ threshold given a high $\eta_i$.
L000282	Importantly, the $C_{1,\theta}$ term occurs when the diamagnetic polarization, i.e.~finite Larmor radius (FLR) term $\nabla\cdot (\partial_t+\mathbf{u}_{E} \cdot \nabla ) \mathbf{u}_{*}$ is retained in the vorticity equation.
L000283	Its inclusion introduces a significant real frequency component $\omega_r=\omega_{\mathrm{kbm}}<0$ consistent with \cite{aleynikova2017quantitative}.
L000284	Consequently, when KBM is marginally unstable, we expect $\alpha_{\phi,n}\approx \pi$ for $\gamma \ll \omega_{\mathrm{kbm}}$.
L000285	The significance of FLR physics in the KBM turbulence is further substantiated by performing a test branched from the reference case, wherein we disable the term $\nabla\cdot(\mathbf{u}_{E} \cdot \nabla \mathbf{u}_{*})$.
L000286	Fig.~\ref{fig:self}(b) compares the cross-coherence between $\tilde{\phi}$ and $\tilde{n}$ at $\rpol=0.999$ near the OMP for the reference case and the no-FLR test.
L000287	The probability density increases linearly from the outer to the inner contours.
L000288	When FLR is removed, the picture shifts from a coherent pattern aligned diagonally  ($\alpha_{\phi,n}\approx \pi$) to broadband turbulence aligned horizontally ($\alpha_{\phi,n}\approx \pi/2$).
L000289	The no-FLR case, subject to interchange-like dynamics, exhibits a tenfold increase in $E\times B$ heat flux, leading to pedestal collapse and thus discrepancy with the experiment.
L000290	These results underscore that FLR stabilization is essential for sustaining the coherent QCM structure with $\alpha_{\phi,n}\approx \pi$, and for preventing its transport from degrading the confinement.
L000291	
L000292	{To summarize, validated simulations reveal the turbulent nature of the Quasicontinuous Exhaust regime, a promising scenario for fusion reactors.
L000293	The experimentally observed QCM and blob dynamics, reproduced in the simulations, are governed by two separatrix-spanning modes.
L000294	The QCM is a kinetic ballooning mode (KBM) that develops an extended, mesoscopic radial correlation length via electromagnetic self-organization of turbulence, thereby driving enhanced transport.
L000295	Blob launching occurs via interchange dynamics when this upstream KBM activity interacts with a resistive mode originating from the X-point region (RXM).}
L000296	
L000297	{This work provides a sound physics basis for extrapolations to future reactors.
L000298	Our results suggest, first, that access to the KBM-dominated QCM should depend primarily on the beta-related ballooning parameter \cite{dunne2024quasi,faitsch2025quasi}, rather than on collisionality-related parameters \cite{Zeiler1998,scott2005drift,eich2020turbulence} as assumed in extrapolations based on resistivity-dominated modes \cite{faitsch2023analysis}.
L000299	However, the experimentally observed separatrix density dependence \cite{faitsch2023analysis,miller2025determination} may instead reflect the requirement for reduced $E\times B$ shear.
L000300	Quantitatively predicting whether QCM transport is sufficient to suppress ELMs, yet not so excessive as to degrade confinement, will require parameterizing the $E\times B$ shear weakening by Maxwell stress and interchange stabilization by finite-Larmor-radius physics, respectively.
L000301	Second, blob-enhanced exhaust requires the coexistence of those KBMs with an RXM destabilized by elevated local collisionality near the X-point, rather than the excitation of a resistive ballooning mode by high upstream collisionality.
L000302	Thus, despite the more constrained access to high outboard-midplane separatrix collisionality in reactor-scale devices such as ITER \cite{labit2019dependence,faitsch2025quasi}, the mechanisms elucidated herein may nevertheless remain applicable for reconciling high confinement with effective heat exhaust in future fusion plasmas.
